{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on ed5f919b, the R15 signer and a refused revoke.\n\n(1) Include a new membership revision in R15. It admits one more revoke: a key revokes itself, with as: retired. The signing key and the revoked key are the same key, and the act names no other key. Everything else stays as it is: revoke-key by a membership.manage holder or by the recovery key works as today, and the recovery key's rules do not change. Retirement then never waits on an admin.\n\nThe hosted retire uses R15's first option, the agent's own key:\na. Retire first disables the agent at the endpoint: bearer cleared, sessions deleted, and no control signs as the agent.\nb. The endpoint signs exactly one revoke-key for the agent's key with the agent's seed.\nc. In one storage transaction, the endpoint stores that exact envelope and erases the seed. After that, nothing in the retire path needs the seed, because a retry resubmits the stored envelope and never signs a second one. This replaces \"seed erasure only after recorded revocation\" in your conditions: holding the seed longer only extends its exposure.\nd. The endpoint submits the stored envelope. Retire reports complete when membership shows the key revoked and the store holds no seed.\n\nThe retire body carries no controller-signed envelope. Whoever may retire the agent today, an admin or its ordinary controller, still may.\n\n(2) Yes. If the stored revoke is permanently refused or expires before acceptance, the retire stays pending. Its answer names the refusal or the expiry, the endpoint stays disabled, and the seed is already gone. A retry reports the same state and never signs a different revoke. The recovery path is a revoke-key of that key by a membership.manage holder or by the recovery key. When membership shows the key revoked by any recorded act, the next retry reports the retire complete. The same holds in a room whose membership revision predates self-revoke: the self-revoke is refused there, and the recovery path applies.\n\nA retry completes the retire only when the revoke was uncertain or refused for a passing reason. Read R15 item 3 and its refused-revoke witness that way: the witness uses an uncertain or passing refusal, and a second witness shows a permanent refusal staying pending until an admin revoke.\n\nThe head report names the new membership revision and its pin. docs/mcp.md says what retire does to the key, the seed and the record, and what a pending retire means."}